of input communities, and \"moves\" is a list of moves that would be made if we apply some optimization, but final communities are not updated. But that contradicts the schema requiring dynamic keys from case.communities.keys() - it might be that the output communities is the input communities because they want to verify that we correctly copy them. But then why have moves? Probably we need to update communities.\n\nGiven the schema explicitly says dynamicKeysFrom case.communities.keys(), it means the output communities must have exactly the same set of keys (node names) as the input communities, but values can be different. So we update values.\n\nThus, we will implement a function that, given case (payload), does:\n\n- Extract edges, communities, resolution.\n- Build adjacency list (node -> list of (neighbor, weight)).\n- Build current community mapping from input.\n- Build community sizes: count of nodes per community.\n- For each node in sorted order:\n  - Compute current community comm_current.\n  - For each neighbor community (set of communities of neighbors, plus possibly own community? but own gives gain 0, skip) compute gain if node moves to that community.\n  - Compute w_internal_current = sum of weights to neighbors in same community (excluding self).\n  - For each candidate community B (different from current), compute w_internal_target = sum of weights to neighbors in B.\n  - delta = w_internal_target - w_internal_current - resolution * (size_B - (size_current - 1))\n  - If delta > 0 and is the best so far (max), record move.\n  - After checking all candidates, if best > 0, apply move: update node's community to best target, update sizes (decrease size_current, increase size_target), record move object {\"from\": current_comm, \"node\": node_name, \"to\": target_comm}.\n- After processing all nodes, return {\"communities\": updated_communities (node->final community string), \"moves\": list of moves}\n\nNote: The move recording should be in order of exe